WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:03.110
[Research on conjoined twins part 1]

00:00:03.110 --> 00:00:07.260
[The USSR Academy of Medical Sciences]

00:00:07.260 --> 00:00:19.100
[Neural and humoral factors in the regulation of bodily functions (research on conjoined twins)]

00:00:19.100 --> 00:00:26.170
[Research director, full member of the USSR Academy of Medical Sciences professor P.K. Anokhin]

00:00:26.170 --> 00:00:33.230
[Basic scientific research performed by Candidate of Medical Sciences T.T. Alekseeva]

00:00:33.230 --> 00:00:36.140
[Director N.P. Il'zina

00:00:36.140 --> 00:00:38.230
Operator N.A. Arkhipov

00:00:38.230 --> 00:00:42.050
Animator L.IA. Kal']

00:00:42.050 --> 00:00:45.160
[Department of Scientific Film and Photo Documentation 

00:00:45.160 --> 00:00:48.260
of the USSR Academy of Medical Sciences, 1957]

00:00:48.260 --> 00:00:52.000
[The role of neural and humoral factors in the solution 

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:58.000
of certain physiological problems remained unknown,

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:05.140
because in the conditions of the experiment each of them was assessed separately.]

00:01:05.140 --> 00:01:06.750
[Conjoined twins are being demonstrated.]

00:01:06.750 --> 00:01:09.010
[Narrator:] The role of neural and humoral factors in the solution 

00:01:09.010 --> 00:01:12.000
of certain physiological problems remained unknown,

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:15.000
due to the conditions of the experiment, 

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:22.000
where each of them was assessed separately.

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:25.000
[A bath is drawn and water temperature is measured.]

00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:29.000
[Narrator:] Due to their unique physiological properties,

00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:33.000
conjoined twins are extremely rare objects for solving controversial questions

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:35.000
regarding the regulation of bodily functions of organisms, 

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:42.000
in which the interrelationship of neural and humoral mechanisms seemed the most complicated.

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:46.000
[The conjoined twins are given a bath in a small basin.]

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:52.000
[Narrator:] Scarce descriptions of conjoined twins in the world literature address primarily 

00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:57.000
their external appearance and anatomical structure, 

00:01:57.000 --> 00:02:01.000
since most of them were stillborn.

00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:05.000
[The conjoined twins are towel-dried and swaddled.] 

00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:07.000
[A nurse holds the babies in her arms, looks down at them and smiles.]

00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:11.500
[The conjoined twins are lying in bed, facing each other.]

00:02:11.500 --> 00:02:13.000
[The X-ray photograph of the conjoined twins in supine position is shown.]

00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:16.000
[Narrator:] In 1937, in one of the maternity hospitals of the Soviet Union, 

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:20.000
the conjoined twins Ira-Galia were born.

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:23.000
The twins survived one year and twenty-two days. 

00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:26.000
The anatomical and physiological features of the twins 

00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:30.000
were studied while they were still alive.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:32.000
[Close-up X-ray photo of the conjoined twins' pelvis area is shown.]

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:37.000
[Narrator:] The X-ray photograph shows two spines.

00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:41.000
[Back to the original X-ray supine position photo of the conjoined twins.]

00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:43.500
[X-ray photo changes to image of twins in prone position.]

00:02:43.500 --> 00:02:47.000
[Narrator:] The coccyx sections of the spines were fused together;

00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:52.000
however, the spinal canals remained isolated.

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:57.000
[X-ray photo of the conjoined twins with the highlighted spinal cords 

00:02:57.000 --> 00:03:03.000
and peripheral nervous system is shown.]

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:06.000
[Narrator:] Respectively, the structures of the vertebral canals 

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:11.000
of the twins' neural system were entirely separated from each other.

00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:16.000
[Close-up X-ray photo of the spinal nerves.]

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:21.000
[Narrator:] At the point of fusion, the nerve roots of the inner sides of the spine

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:28.000
are joined into a rudimental tail. 

00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:34.000
These nerves, lacking an innervation zone due to the absence of the two extremities, 

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:38.000
are significantly thinner than the nerves of the outer side of the spine.

00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:39.000
[X-ray photo demonstrates a vertebra where the nerves are very thin, 

00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:40.000
then changes back to a close-up of the X-ray photo of the spinal nerves.]

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:46.000
[Narrator:] Microscopic studies conducted by Rampan showed that 

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:53.000
the segments of the cervical and thoracic sections have a normal structure.

00:03:53.000 --> 00:03:56.000
[X-ray photo demonstrates normally developed vertebrae.]

00:03:56.000 --> 00:04:00.000
[Narrator:] Inside the lumbar section of the spinal cord, 

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:02.000
the grey matter of the anterior horns, 

00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:06.000
corresponding to the absent extremities, is stunted.

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:10.000
[The conjoined twins are shown in bed in supine position.]

00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:12.000
[X-ray photograph of the twins' upper body is shown.]

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:21.000
[Narrator:] The number of ganglion cells in these areas of the spinal cord is limited.

00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:24.000
[The conjoined twins are shown in bed in supine position.]

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:29.000
[Narrator:] In contrast to the nervous system, the twins' blood circulation was common. 

00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:33.000
The blood of one child could freely flow to the other,

00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:38.000
as was apparent from the venous net, visible through the abdominal skin.

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:45.000
[X-ray photo of the conjoined twins with highlighted circulatory system.]

00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:50.000
[Narrator:] The common bloodstream, besides the sub-dermic venous system, 

00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:53.000
can be seen as well in the system of abdominal aortas, 

00:04:53.000 --> 00:05:01.000
where the direct arterial anastomoses were located.

00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:04.000
[A picture of the conjoined twins' abdominal vascular system is shown.]

00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:07.000
[Narrator:] The post-mortem, performed by professor Shangina, 

00:05:07.000 --> 00:05:13.000
demonstrated a close vascular connection in almost of all the internal organs' blood vessels.

00:05:13.000 --> 00:05:16.000
[Swaddled newborn conjoined twins are shown sleeping, then uncovered and awake, then crying.]

00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:19.000
[Narrator:] For example, the veins of both intestinal systems join together

00:05:19.000 --> 00:05:28.000
and the mixed blood flows to the liver through the separate portal veins.

00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:35.000
[Narrator:] In 1950 another pair of conjoined twins was born, Masha-Dasha.

00:05:35.000 --> 00:05:36.000
[Newborn naked conjoined twins are shown.]

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:37.000
[Then the X-ray photograph of the conjoined twins is displayed, 

00:05:37.000 --> 00:05:38.000
followed by a close-up of the third hip joint.]

00:05:38.000 --> 00:05:44.000
[Narrator:] They had two torsos, four arms. 

00:05:44.000 --> 00:05:51.000
The twins had two regular legs and a third one, 

00:05:51.000 --> 00:05:56.000
made out of two legs fused together. 

00:05:56.000 --> 00:06:03.000
An X-ray also demonstrated that these girls had two completely separate spines, 

00:06:03.000 --> 00:06:08.000
down to the coccyx bones.

00:06:08.000 --> 00:06:10.000
[A picture of the conjoined twins' highlighted nervous systems is shown.]

00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:16.000
[Narrator:] Their spinal canals are separate as well.

00:06:16.000 --> 00:06:20.000
The nervous system of these twins was also divided

00:06:20.000 --> 00:06:25.000
according to the location of the spinal canals.

00:06:25.000 --> 00:06:27.000
The innervation of the arms and the regular legs 

00:06:27.000 --> 00:06:32.000
corresponded to the usual anatomically normal ratios.

00:06:32.000 --> 00:06:36.000
The innervation of the fused leg is divided. 

00:06:36.000 --> 00:06:42.000
Its nerves proceed from the spinal cord of each girl.

00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:47.000
[Newborn naked sleeping conjoined twins are shown.]

00:06:47.000 --> 00:06:51.000
[Narrator:] The blood circulation of the second twin pair Masha-Dasha, 

00:06:51.000 --> 00:06:57.000
similarly to the blood circulation of Ira-Galia, was common. 

00:06:57.000 --> 00:07:02.000
Anastomosis of the venous net is also visible through the abdominal skin.

00:07:02.000 --> 00:07:05.000
[Glucose loading table is drawn, using multiplication technique.]

00:07:05.000 --> 00:07:08.000
[Narrator:] The rapidity of blood transfer from one child to the other

00:07:08.000 --> 00:07:12.000
was calculated with the help of a glucose-loading experiment, 

00:07:12.000 --> 00:07:15.000
which was conducted in both pairs of twins. 

00:07:15.000 --> 00:07:20.000
The amount of sugar before loading is estimated. 

00:07:20.000 --> 00:07:26.000
100 ml of glucose is injected into Ira's scalp vein. 

00:07:26.000 --> 00:07:29.000
In less than two minutes after the injection, 

00:07:29.000 --> 00:07:38.000
the blood glucose count is high in both of the girls. 

00:07:38.000 --> 00:07:46.000
Similar results were obtained during the glucose loading in the Masha-Dasha twins.

00:07:46.000 --> 00:07:52.000
[Table of thyroid assimilation of iodine is drawn, using multiplication technique.]

00:07:52.000 --> 00:07:59.000
[Narrator:] Additional radioactive iodine experiments were conducted on Masha-Dasha.

00:07:59.000 --> 00:08:02.000
After Masha had taken radioactive iodine orally, 

00:08:02.000 --> 00:08:08.000
the iodine absorption by the thyroid glands in both girls occurred in 10 minutes. 

00:08:08.000 --> 00:08:15.000
The above indicates close humoral interaction between both twin organisms,

00:08:15.000 --> 00:08:21.000
and that practically all substances are equally distributed in a common blood flow.

00:08:21.000 --> 00:08:23.000
[The newborn conjoined twins are lying in bed, naked.]

00:08:23.000 --> 00:08:25.000
[A nurse repeatedly pricks the twins, checking their reaction.]

00:08:25.000 --> 00:08:27.000
[Narrator:] Receptor zone boundaries 

00:08:27.000 --> 00:08:32.000
and the degree of their isolation were studied in both pairs of twins. 

00:08:32.000 --> 00:08:38.000
Light irritation in the innervation area, located 1 cm from the navel in Ira's direction, 

00:08:38.000 --> 00:08:45.000
causes a reaction only from Ira. 

00:08:45.000 --> 00:08:49.000
The same irritation, located 1 cm from the navel in Galia's direction, 

00:08:49.000 --> 00:08:54.000
causes a reaction only from Galia. 

00:08:54.000 --> 00:08:58.000
The above indicates that the median line of the skin is the accurate border 

00:08:58.000 --> 00:09:05.000
of innervation by dorsal root filaments from both spinal cords.

00:09:05.000 --> 00:09:06.500
[The nurse gives the conjoined twins an injection, 

00:09:06.500 --> 00:09:08.000
first in one leg, then in the other leg.]

00:09:08.000 --> 00:09:11.000
[First one twin, then the second twin cries.]

00:09:11.000 --> 00:09:15.000
[Narrator:] Injections of pharmaceutical preparations were conducted for therapeutic purposes.

00:09:15.000 --> 00:09:21.000
Only the girl who was receiving the injection in her leg reacted by crying. 

00:09:21.000 --> 00:09:24.000
The other girl remained calm.

00:09:24.000 --> 00:09:26.000
[Narrator:] The above observation is not consistent 

00:09:26.000 --> 00:09:29.000
with the theory of the humoral nature of pain factors.

00:09:29.000 --> 00:09:39.000
[View of the twins from above.]

00:09:39.000 --> 00:09:42.000
[Narrator:] In addition to the separate sensitivity zones, 

00:09:42.000 --> 00:09:46.000
extensor muscle apparatuses were divided as well. 

00:09:46.000 --> 00:09:52.000
Each girl could arbitrarily manipulate only one leg--her own.

00:09:52.000 --> 00:09:56.000
[Newborn Masha-Dasha twins are lying in bed, naked.]

00:09:56.000 --> 00:10:03.000
[A nurse pricks first one twin, then the second one, checking their reaction.]

00:10:03.000 --> 00:10:09.000
[Narrator:] The second pair of twins also displayed full isolation of receptor zones.

00:10:09.000 --> 00:10:18.000
During the light irritation in the navel area in Masha's direction, only Masha was crying.

00:10:18.000 --> 00:10:26.000
The skin irritation near the navel in Dasha's direction resulted in crying only from Dasha.

00:10:26.000 --> 00:10:28.000
[Nurse alternately applies pressure to the bottom of each foot.]

00:10:28.000 --> 00:10:38.000
[First only Dasha, then only Masha is crying.]

00:10:38.000 --> 00:10:46.000
[Narrator:] The legs, in exactly the same way, belonged one to Dasha, the other to Masha.

00:10:46.000 --> 00:10:52.000
[Close-up of crying Masha.]

00:10:52.000 --> 00:10:58.000
[Narrator:] The nerves in the third leg ran side by side without anastomosing.

00:10:58.000 --> 00:11:01.000
The observations of both pairs of twins have allowed us 

00:11:01.000 --> 00:11:08.000
to establish a significant similarity in their physiological functions. 

00:11:08.000 --> 00:11:12.000
The full isolation of the nervous apparatus 

00:11:12.000 --> 00:11:17.000
and the commonality of humoral connections determined all the differences 

00:11:17.000 --> 00:11:21.000
in the behavior and body reactions of the twins.

00:11:21.000 --> 00:11:24.000
[The twins are active, then one girl is asleep while the other is playing with a rattle.]

00:11:24.000 --> 00:11:27.000
[Narrator:] The tests, conducted in exactly the same conditions of the environment 

00:11:27.000 --> 00:11:29.000
and the same routine, demonstrated that the twins' development rates were 

00:11:29.000 --> 00:11:34.000
unequal when it came to their behavior, 

00:11:34.000 --> 00:11:37.000
static and motor skills development,

00:11:37.000 --> 00:11:42.000
and to the acquisition of conditioned responses. 

00:11:42.000 --> 00:11:47.000
Ira and Galia differed little from each other in their physical condition,

00:11:47.000 --> 00:11:54.000
but Ira surpassed Galia in her mental development. 

00:11:54.000 --> 00:12:03.000
She was the first to learn to sit up, and she was the first to smile as well.

00:12:03.000 --> 00:12:07.000
[One of the twins reaches for the nurse's hands, smiles.]

00:12:07.000 --> 00:12:15.000
[Narrator:] Individual differences are also evident in Masha's and Dasha's behavior.

00:12:15.000 --> 00:12:17.000
[Twins are swaddled, nurse is shaking a rattle over one of the girls.]

00:12:17.000 --> 00:12:20.000
[Narrator:] In the first months of their lives this difference was minimally expressed. 

00:12:20.000 --> 00:12:30.000
At the age of three months both girls weren't able to hold their heads. 

00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:36.000
Still, object fixation in that age is better expressed in Masha.

00:12:36.000 --> 00:12:40.000
She independently captures a toy.

00:12:40.000 --> 00:12:42.000
[The twins, a little older, both in patterned dresses and white bibs, are active.]

00:12:42.000 --> 00:12:45.000
[A large rattle is hanging over the cradle.]

00:12:45.000 --> 00:12:46.000
[Masha is holding a toy parrot in her hand and is vigorously waving it around.]

00:12:46.000 --> 00:12:47.000
[A nurse shows a knitted sock to Masha, who first tries to reach it, than gives up.]

00:12:47.000 --> 00:12:49.000
[A nurse puts a sock on her foot.]

00:12:49.000 --> 00:12:53.000
[When the nurse moves to put the other sock on Dasha's foot, she actively interacts with her.]

00:12:53.000 --> 00:12:56.000
[Narrator:] However, later, due to her tendency to get tired easily,

00:12:56.000 --> 00:13:05.000
Masha began to fall behind Dasha in her development. 

00:13:05.000 --> 00:13:11.000
At the age of 18 months Dasha accurately performed elementary tasks, 

00:13:11.000 --> 00:13:21.000
willingly raised her leg up when the sock was pulled on.

00:13:21.000 --> 00:13:30.000
Masha wouldn't perform as requested; she was distracted by the surrounding scene.

00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:41.000
[One of the twins is shown playing with her leg, while a nurse is talking to her.]

00:13:41.000 --> 00:13:47.000
[Narrator:] Dasha also moved the joint leg more, and, when asked, raised it up.

00:13:47.000 --> 00:13:53.000
[Nurse is giving spoons to the twins, Dasha takes a spoon and starts playing.]

00:13:53.000 --> 00:14:01.000
[Masha reacts more slowly, doesn't play, and is visibly distracted by the camera.]

00:14:01.000 --> 00:14:06.000
[Narrator:] The children's tasks became more complicated with age. 

00:14:06.000 --> 00:14:10.000
The twins' attitude to various items was becoming apparent.

00:14:10.000 --> 00:14:14.000
Masha was able to part with the toys and other things without a protest. 

00:14:14.000 --> 00:14:17.000
In response to a request she at once surrenders a spoon

00:14:17.000 --> 00:14:22.000
and quickly switches her attention to other items.

00:14:22.000 --> 00:14:25.000
[Dasha is very protective of her spoon.]

00:14:25.000 --> 00:14:31.000
[She clutches it, screams, tries to get it back, and succeeds.]

00:14:31.000 --> 00:14:38.000
[Narrator:] Dasha has a pronounced active-defensive reaction. 

00:14:38.000 --> 00:14:46.910
She doesn't comply with the request, and keeps the spoon.

00:14:46.910 --> 00:15:01.360
[Nurse gives each of the girls a cookie, than takes it away from Masha, who actively resists.]

00:15:01.360 --> 00:15:06.000
[Narrator:] The same reaction appears in Masha when she is given sweets. 

00:15:06.000 --> 00:15:13.000
She is very fond of cookies and reluctant to part with them.

00:15:13.000 --> 00:15:17.000
[Dasha pulls a sock on her leg. Masha is very reluctant to even take a sock into her hands.]

00:15:17.000 --> 00:15:20.000
[Finally she takes it, starts pulling it on, but gets distracted and throws it away.]

00:15:20.000 --> 00:15:27.000
[Narrator:] At the age of two Dasha acquired her initial labor skills;

00:15:27.000 --> 00:15:37.000
she independently started to pull a stocking on her leg. 

00:15:37.000 --> 00:15:57.000
It's difficult to persuade Masha to even take a stocking in her hand. 

00:15:57.000 --> 00:16:12.000
She unsuccessfully attempts to pull a stocking on, then gets rid of it.

00:16:12.000 --> 00:16:14.000
[A nurse attempts to give a wrapped candy to Masha, but she doesn't take it.

00:16:14.000 --> 00:16:15.000
At the same time Dasha takes her candy, unwraps both layers,

00:16:15.000 --> 00:16:17.000
hands the wrappers back to the nurse, and puts the candy in her mouth. 

00:16:17.000 --> 00:16:18.000
When Masha finally takes her wrapped candy, she isn't sure what to do with it.]

00:16:18.000 --> 00:16:21.000
[Narrator:] At a more advanced age, 

00:16:21.000 --> 00:16:28.000
Dasha performed differentiated movements with the utmost precision. 

00:16:28.000 --> 00:16:45.000
She unwraps a candy quickly and skillfully. 

00:16:45.000 --> 00:16:51.000
Masha doesn't even make an attempt to unwrap her candy.

00:16:51.000 --> 00:16:54.000
[The twins are playing with blocks. Dasha builds a tower.]

00:16:54.000 --> 00:17:02.000
[Masha prefers to scatter the blocks instead of building a structure herself.]

00:17:02.000 --> 00:17:10.000
[Narrator:] Dasha quickly puts the building blocks together.

00:17:10.000 --> 00:17:14.000
Masha would rather destroy the constructions 

00:17:14.000 --> 00:17:20.000
instead of attempting to build anything herself.

00:17:20.000 --> 00:17:21.000
[The twins are shown in an open crib.]

00:17:21.000 --> 00:17:23.000
[A nurse comes, greets them with a handshake, 

00:17:23.000 --> 00:17:29.000
and hands a rubber hose ending in a small balloon to each of them.]

00:17:29.000 --> 00:17:35.000
[Narrator:] Differences in neuro-psychological reactions were confirmed in laboratory conditions. 

00:17:35.000 --> 00:17:39.000
Motor-conditioned responses are developed. 

00:17:39.000 --> 00:17:44.000
The research was conducted by Alekseeva, Kriuchkova, and Ostrovskaia. 

00:17:44.000 --> 00:17:49.000
In response to the word 'press' the children must press the rubber balloon with their hand. 

00:17:49.000 --> 00:17:52.000
The word 'press' is preceded by conditioning signals, a ring, and a light.

00:17:52.000 --> 00:17:53.000
[A nurse is sitting at a desk adjacent to the crib.]

00:17:53.000 --> 00:17:54.000
[She is conducting the experiment by moving levers on a control panel.]

00:17:54.000 --> 00:17:58.000
[Narrator:] It is presumed that the conditioned response is developed

00:17:58.000 --> 00:18:10.000
when the girls press the balloon directly after the ring or light signal.

00:18:10.000 --> 00:18:12.000
The bell rings.

00:18:12.000 --> 00:18:15.000
[A bell is ringing.]

00:18:15.000 --> 00:18:26.000
[Narrator:] Dasha is the first to press the balloon. The top second curve marks Masha's movements.

00:18:26.000 --> 00:18:28.000
[A close-up of an apparatus recording the twins' conditioned movement reflexes on paper is shown,

00:18:28.000 --> 00:18:30.000
then the picture switches back to the nurse at the desk.]

00:18:30.000 --> 00:18:39.000
[Narrator:] Signal light.The first to respond to the stimulus is Dasha (top recording)

00:18:39.000 --> 00:18:41.000
[Masha is tired. She throws the ball away. The nurse pulls a lever again.]

00:18:41.000 --> 00:18:42.000
[A bell is ringing. Masha has both balloons in her hands.]

00:18:42.000 --> 00:18:49.000
[Dasha takes her balloon and presses it. She is the first again.]

00:18:49.000 --> 00:18:57.000
[Narrator:] Signal again. Again, Dasha is the first to press [the balloon]. 

00:18:57.000 --> 00:19:05.000
Masha is tired, she discards the balloon and doesn't want to continue. 

00:19:05.000 --> 00:19:07.000
At this point the task diversifies.

00:19:07.000 --> 00:19:10.000
Both balloons are in Masha's hands.

00:19:10.000 --> 00:19:15.000
Signal sounds. Dasha snatches her balloon, presses. 

00:19:15.000 --> 00:19:20.000
Therefore, Dasha developed the conditioned response faster.

00:19:20.000 --> 00:19:28.000
[The twins play with rubber balls.]

00:19:28.000 --> 00:19:34.000
[Narrator:] Comparing the children's behavior with the conditioned responses, 

00:19:34.000 --> 00:19:42.000
one can conclude that the twins noticeably differed in their higher nervous activity, 

00:19:42.000 --> 00:19:47.000
i.e., each one had her own typological features.

00:19:47.000 --> 00:19:49.000
[Differentiation of nervous systems and the commonality of blood circulation 

00:19:49.000 --> 00:19:51.000
in twins presented an opportunity to reveal the role of 

00:19:51.000 --> 00:19:58.000
nervous and humoral factors in the occurrence of sleep.]

00:19:58.000 --> 00:20:01.000
[Newborn conjoined twins are shown, first active, then both asleep.]

00:20:01.000 --> 00:20:05.000
[Narrator:] Differentiation of nervous systems and the commonality of blood circulation

00:20:05.000 --> 00:20:08.000
in twins presented an opportunity to discover 

00:20:08.000 --> 00:20:17.000
the role of nervous and humoral factors in the occurrence of sleep. 

00:20:17.000 --> 00:20:25.000
Most of the time, the girls' sleep was simultaneous. 

00:20:25.000 --> 00:20:28.000
However, they were able to sleep independently, 

00:20:28.000 --> 00:20:35.000
and such opposite physiological states "the sleep of one and the wakefulness of the other" 

00:20:35.000 --> 00:20:40.000
could continue for a lengthy period of time, up to two hours and more.

00:20:40.000 --> 00:20:42.000
[One twin is shown awake, while the other is asleep.]

00:20:42.000 --> 00:20:45.000
[Narrator:] The states of sleep and wakefulness in the first pair of twins, Ira-Galia, 

00:20:45.000 --> 00:20:55.000
also were occurring both simultaneously and separately.

00:20:55.000 --> 00:21:01.000
Ira is asleep, Galia is awake.

00:21:01.000 --> 00:21:09.000
Galia is asleep, Ira is awake.

00:21:09.000 --> 00:21:13.000
Galia is laughing, Ira is asleep.

00:21:13.000 --> 00:21:28.000
[Pneumograms of sleep/wake patterns of both pairs of conjoined twins are presented.]

00:21:28.000 --> 00:21:32.000
[Narrator:] All the autonomic nervous system indicators 

00:21:32.000 --> 00:21:38.000
during sleep and wakefulness changed in accordance with the condition of each child. 

00:21:38.000 --> 00:21:45.000
During simultaneous sleep, the twins' breathing was synchronized. 

00:21:45.000 --> 00:21:50.000
During individual sleep, the pneumogram's recording changed respectively,

00:21:50.000 --> 00:21:55.000
the breathing of the wakeful child was always more energetic,

00:21:55.000 --> 00:21:58.000
i.e., deeper and more frequent. 

00:21:58.000 --> 00:22:04.000
The heartbeat of the wakeful child was faster, 

00:22:04.000 --> 00:22:10.000
the individual spikes of the electrocardiogram higher in comparison to the sleeping child.

00:22:10.000 --> 00:22:12.000
[The twins, one asleep, one awake, are shown.]

00:22:12.000 --> 00:22:15.000
[Narrator:] With age, the children's sleep and wakefulness

00:22:15.000 --> 00:22:24.000
continued to be independent states for each child.

00:22:24.000 --> 00:22:41.000
[The conjoined twins Masha and Dasha are shown sleeping.]

00:22:41.000 --> 00:22:47.000
[Narrator:] The girls turned five.

00:22:47.000 --> 00:22:53.000
Due to the same schedule, most of the time the children slept simultaneously. 

00:22:53.000 --> 00:22:57.000
In these cases, sleep occurred as a result of the conditioned responses. 

00:22:57.000 --> 00:23:05.000
When one of the children calmed down, it worked as a conditioning signal to the other child, 

00:23:05.000 --> 00:23:16.000
and she would fall asleep as well.

00:23:16.000 --> 00:23:32.000
[Masha is shown playing, while Dasha is asleep.]

00:23:32.000 --> 00:23:38.000
[Narrator:] The simultaneous existence of both states 'sleep and wakefulness'

00:23:38.000 --> 00:23:43.000
in conjunction with common blood circulation and separate nervous systems

00:23:43.000 --> 00:23:51.000
is direct proof of the crucial role of the nervous system in sleep occurrence, 

00:23:51.000 --> 00:23:55.000
which speaks against the humoral theory of sleep.

00:23:55.000 --> 00:23:56.000
[End of part 1.]

00:23:56.000 --> 00:23:57.000
[Research on conjoined twins part 2]

00:23:57.000 --> 00:23:58.000
[The role of neural and humoral factors in alimentary stimulation.]

00:23:58.000 --> 00:24:00.000
[The X-ray photograph of the conjoined twins in supine position is shown.]

00:24:00.000 --> 00:24:06.000
[Narrator:] The role of neural and humoral factors in alimentary stimulation. 

00:24:06.000 --> 00:24:09.000
The existence of separate stomachs in the twins created the conditions 

00:24:09.000 --> 00:24:12.000
for the normal nutrition of each child. 

00:24:12.000 --> 00:24:18.000
Gastric digestion was occurring separately, the same as in the duodenum. 

00:24:18.000 --> 00:24:24.500
However, absorbing digestive juices were entering the common bloodstream.

00:24:24.500 --> 00:24:27.000
[The conjoined twins are shown in a crib, swaddled. A nurse bottle-feeds them.]

00:24:27.000 --> 00:24:28.000
[Narrator:] Based on these anatomical and physiological peculiarities, 

00:24:28.000 --> 00:24:36.000
observations of alimentary stimulation in twins were conducted.

00:24:36.000 --> 00:24:43.000
During the first six months of life, the children received food every two to three hours. 

00:24:43.000 --> 00:24:48.000
They were always fed at the same time and were given equal portions. 

00:24:48.000 --> 00:25:05.000
Each girl was drinking up to 75 ml of milk in one feeding.

00:25:05.000 --> 00:25:13.000
[One of the conjoined twins, approximately six months old,is spoon-fed, while the other twin is crying.] 

00:25:13.000 --> 00:25:15.000
[Narrator:] Study of alimentary stimulation was conducted 

00:25:15.000 --> 00:25:20.000
by means of separate feeding of one of the twins. 

00:25:20.000 --> 00:25:23.000
The blood sugar and the gastric juice composition 

00:25:23.000 --> 00:25:29.000
of the child not receiving food were measured. 

00:25:29.000 --> 00:25:39.000
The study consisted of one child being fed, while the other was not.

00:25:39.000 --> 00:25:53.000
[An attempt to distract the crying twin with a rattle.]

00:25:53.000 --> 00:25:55.000
[Table labeled 'Amount of food ingested during separate feedings' is drawn, 

00:25:55.000 --> 00:25:58.000
first for Ira-Galia, then for Masha-Dasha.]  

00:25:58.000 --> 00:26:05.000
[Narrator:] The same studies were conducted also on the first pair of twins, Ira-Galia. 

00:26:05.000 --> 00:26:13.000
The amount of milk consumed by each of the girls in one feeding is recorded.

00:26:13.000 --> 00:26:21.000
Next time Galia was not given milk (it is shown as a dash on the diagram). 

00:26:21.000 --> 00:26:33.000
After two skipped feedings it was found out that Galia was drinking a larger amount of milk. 

00:26:33.000 --> 00:26:46.000
Similar results were received also from the Masha-Dasha twins. 

00:26:46.000 --> 00:26:55.000
After two skipped feedings Dasha was drinking considerably more milk 

00:26:55.000 --> 00:27:00.000
than her usual portion during the regular feedings.

00:27:00.000 --> 00:27:10.000
[Dasha is being spoon-fed. She is eating vigorously.]

00:27:10.000 --> 00:27:20.000
[After that both twins look very content.]

00:27:20.000 --> 00:27:22.000
[The conjoined twins are lying in a crib, dressed in small print dresses.]

00:27:22.000 --> 00:27:26.000
[A nurse ties a bib around the twin on the left.]

00:27:26.000 --> 00:27:30.000
[Narrator:] During the first year of life, pronounced alimentary stimulation 

00:27:30.000 --> 00:27:35.000
was observed both during separate and simultaneous feedings. 

00:27:35.000 --> 00:27:39.000
Alimentary stimulation of the child not receiving food is increased 

00:27:39.000 --> 00:27:44.000
by such conditioned response stimuli as preparation for 

00:27:44.000 --> 00:27:50.000
food intake and the feeding of the other child.

00:27:50.000 --> 00:27:56.000
[The twin on the left is spoon-fed.]

00:27:56.000 --> 00:28:03.000
[After the feeding, the nurse takes the soiled bib off the child 

00:28:03.000 --> 00:28:08.000
and ties a clean bib around the other twin.]

00:28:08.000 --> 00:28:27.000
[It is Dasha's turn to be fed.]

00:28:27.000 --> 00:28:30.000
[The twins are in a crib. They are older, perhaps three or four years old.]

00:28:30.000 --> 00:28:32.000
[A nurse comes with two plates of food and gives a plate to each of the girls.] 

00:28:32.000 --> 00:28:34.000
[The girls are eating independently.]

00:28:34.000 --> 00:28:40.000
[Narrator:] The children's behavior during food intake changes only

00:28:40.000 --> 00:28:50.000
with the development of speech communication.

00:28:50.000 --> 00:29:01.000
[Each twin has a tube inserted into her mouth, apparently running to her stomach.]

00:29:01.000 --> 00:29:07.000
[Narrator:] Gastric juice studies were conducted in order to prove 

00:29:07.000 --> 00:29:09.000
that the products of digested nutrients indeed entered the common bloodstream.

00:29:09.000 --> 00:29:11.000
[A comparative table of gastric juice analysis for Masha and Dasha is drawn.] 

00:29:11.000 --> 00:29:13.000
[Gastric juice, free acid, common acidity.]

00:29:13.000 --> 00:29:16.000
[Narrator:] The chemical composition of gastric juice 

00:29:16.000 --> 00:29:19.000
taken from Masha and Dasha after fasting was studied.

00:29:19.000 --> 00:29:26.000
Both gastric juice composition and its strength were the same in both girls.

00:29:26.000 --> 00:29:36.000
[Dasha is spoon-fed, while Masha has a tube inserted for gastric juice analysis.]

00:29:36.000 --> 00:29:45.000
[Narrator:] While one of the girls was being fed, there were observed two-phased changes 

00:29:45.000 --> 00:29:52.000
in the quantity and the acidity of the hungry child's gastric juice.

00:29:52.000 --> 00:29:56.000
[A table 'Gastric juice analysis of Masha (Dasha's being fed)' is drawn.] 

00:29:56.000 --> 00:30:01.000
[Narrator:] The first increase coincides with the neuro-psychiatric phase, 

00:30:01.000 --> 00:30:09.000
while the second with the neuro-chemical phase.

00:30:09.000 --> 00:30:12.000
[A screen is placed between the girls.]

00:30:12.000 --> 00:30:16.000
[The experiment in which Dasha is being fed 

00:30:16.000 --> 00:30:18.000
and Masha's gastric juice is being measured and analyzed, is repeated.]

00:30:18.000 --> 00:30:31.000
[Narrator:] Throughout the course of separate feeding of one girl, 

00:30:31.000 --> 00:30:38.000
the quantity and the acidity of the gastric juice of the child not being fed

00:30:38.000 --> 00:30:43.000
also increases, during both the 'nervous' and the 'chemical' phases. 

00:30:43.000 --> 00:30:49.000
The studies of alimentary stimulation conducted by feeding one of the children separately, 

00:30:49.000 --> 00:30:55.000
together with the simultaneous gastric juice analysis of the fasting child, 

00:30:55.000 --> 00:31:01.000
demonstrate that the alimentary stimulation of one child does not decrease when the other one is fed.

00:31:01.000 --> 00:31:02.000
[The twins are shown in a dining room, sitting at a round table

00:31:02.000 --> 00:31:03.000
covered with a white tablecloth, eating and smiling.]

00:31:03.000 --> 00:31:10.000
[Narrator:] These observations indicate the leading role of the tongue and digestive tract receptors 

00:31:10.000 --> 00:31:20.000
in the regulation of food center activity; in other words, the states of appetite and satiation.

00:31:20.000 --> 00:31:40.000
[The twins continue eating and drinking.]

00:31:40.000 --> 00:31:46.000
[Comparative traits of the twins' pathological conditions.]

00:31:46.000 --> 00:31:48.000
[Narrator:] Comparative traits of the twins' pathological conditions.

00:31:48.000 --> 00:31:53.000
[The twins are lying in bed, covered with a sheet.]

00:31:53.000 --> 00:31:54.000
[A nurse inserts a thermometer under an arm of each girl.]

00:31:54.000 --> 00:31:58.000
[Narrator:] The conducted observational studies of the pathological reactions 

00:31:58.000 --> 00:32:03.000
of the twins revealed the significance of the nervous or humoral factors

00:32:03.000 --> 00:32:09.000
in the processes of the human body's thermoregulation. 

00:32:09.000 --> 00:32:14.000
The role of blood temperature in thermoregulation can be determined

00:32:14.000 --> 00:32:22.000
only by research on conjoined twins, who share a single bloodstream 

00:32:22.000 --> 00:32:24.000
to such a degree that the physicochemical changes in blood 

00:32:24.000 --> 00:32:31.000
over a short period of time 'one to two minutes' are discovered simultaneously in both girls.

00:32:31.000 --> 00:32:34.000
[Close-up of each girl. The nurse returns and removes the thermometers.]

00:32:34.000 --> 00:32:37.000
[Narrator:] In the usual, normal conditions, the body temperature of the twins, 

00:32:37.000 --> 00:32:46.000
both in the armpit and the symmetrical skin folds, is within the range of physiological fluctuations. 

00:32:46.000 --> 00:32:55.000
For example, Dasha's [temperature] is 36.7 [C], Masha's 36.6 [C].

00:32:55.000 --> 00:32:58.000
[A table 'Body temperature during a disease' is drawn.] 

00:32:58.000 --> 00:33:02.000
[Narrator:] Throughout the first year of the children's lives, 

00:33:02.000 --> 00:33:08.000
the body temperature of simultaneously sick girls increased simultaneously.

00:33:08.000 --> 00:33:10.000
[Another temperature table is drawn 'Age 3 months. Masha is sick, Dasha is healthy'.] 

00:33:10.000 --> 00:33:15.000
[Narrator:] Synchronicity of body temperature was observed also during the individual diseases of the twins. 

00:33:15.000 --> 00:33:22.000
At the age of three months, Masha was diagnosed with acute otitis media of the right ear. 

00:33:22.000 --> 00:33:31.000
Dasha was healthy; however, the temperature rose in both girls and stayed elevated for eight days.

00:33:31.000 --> 00:33:34.000
[A temperature table 'Age 2 years 2 months. Masha is sick, Dasha is healthy' is drawn.] 

00:33:34.000 --> 00:33:42.000
[Narrator:] With age, the character of temperature reaction during the pathological conditions changes. 

00:33:42.000 --> 00:33:48.000
At times of individual diseases, the body temperatures do not match. 

00:33:48.000 --> 00:33:51.000
At the age of two Masha gets sick. 

00:33:51.000 --> 00:33:57.000
Her temperature rises to 39 degrees [C], while Dasha's [temperature] does not exceed 37.2 [C].

00:33:57.000 --> 00:33:59.000
[Next temperature table 'Age 3 years. Masha is sick' is drawn.] 

00:33:59.000 --> 00:34:04.000
[Narrator:] At the age of three, Masha fell ill with left-sided pneumonia.

00:34:04.000 --> 00:34:07.000
Dasha had no symptoms of the disease. 

00:34:07.000 --> 00:34:16.000
Masha's temperature rose to 39 degrees [C], while Dasha's temperature did not rise above 37 degrees [C].

00:34:16.000 --> 00:34:18.000
[A hospital bed with Masha and Dasha is shown.]

00:34:18.000 --> 00:34:20.000
[Anurse is measuring skin surface temperature with a special apparatus.] 

00:34:20.000 --> 00:34:24.000
[Narrator:] The skin temperatures also were measured simultaneously 

00:34:24.000 --> 00:34:28.000
in both girls as in normal conditions, as during a disease. 

00:34:28.000 --> 00:34:32.000
The skin temperatures corresponded to the readings of body temperatures. 

00:34:32.000 --> 00:34:36.000
This indicates that the mechanisms of thermoregulation

00:34:36.000 --> 00:34:40.000
are perfected with the children's age,

00:34:40.000 --> 00:34:43.000
and that the blood temperature does not have crucial 

00:34:43.000 --> 00:34:46.000
significance for the determination of body temperature level.

00:34:46.000 --> 00:34:48.500
[Observations conducted on the conjoined twins lead to the conclusion

00:34:48.500 --> 00:34:50.000
that the processes of sleep, disease, hunger, and satiation develop

00:34:50.000 --> 00:34:51.000
under the dominating influence of the nervous system.]

00:34:51.000 --> 00:34:55.000
[Narrator:] Observations conducted on the conjoined twins 

00:34:55.000 --> 00:35:01.000
lead us to the conclusion that the processes of sleep, disease, hunger, and satiation develop

00:35:01.000 --> 00:35:04.000
under the dominating influence of the nervous system.

00:35:04.000 --> 00:35:07.000
[The twins, a few months old, are shown being pulled by their hands

00:35:07.000 --> 00:35:10.000
to a sitting position, first one, then the other.]

00:35:10.000 --> 00:35:15.000
[Narrator:] It has become apparent at an early age that Dasha's and Masha's

00:35:15.000 --> 00:35:18.000
development of motor activity had individual differences. 

00:35:18.000 --> 00:35:24.000
Masha was the first to learn how to hold her head up, 

00:35:24.000 --> 00:35:31.000
while Dasha was not able to hold her head steady.

00:35:31.000 --> 00:35:32.000
[The nurse is talking to Dasha, who is actively responding by moving her arms and legs, 

00:35:32.000 --> 00:35:36.000
and moving her entire body from side to side in a rocking motion. Masha is rather passive.]

00:35:36.000 --> 00:35:40.000
[Narrator:] General physical activity, like a rocking movement of the trunk, 

00:35:40.000 --> 00:35:46.000
was more pronounced in Dasha. 

00:35:46.000 --> 00:35:53.000
Dasha was the first to sit up without assistance. 

00:35:53.000 --> 00:35:57.000
At that time Masha did not even attempt to sit up.

00:35:57.000 --> 00:36:01.000
[The twins are in a large playpen. Masha is lying on her back; Dasha is sitting.]

00:36:01.000 --> 00:36:04.000
[Narrator:] When the children began to crawl, 

00:36:04.000 --> 00:36:12.000
it was Dasha who always initiated the movement in the playpen.

00:36:12.000 --> 00:36:16.000
[The twins are crawling inside the playpen; Dasha is leading.]

00:36:16.000 --> 00:36:18.000
[The twins, looking a bit older, move around the playpen in coordinated motion.]

00:36:18.000 --> 00:36:30.000
[Narrator:] The movement coordination started to appear at the age of two and a half.

00:36:30.000 --> 00:36:32.000
[The twins, visibly older, are lying on a bed, 

00:36:32.000 --> 00:36:36.000
then in perfectly coordinated motion sit up, stand up, look out of the window,

00:36:36.000 --> 00:36:40.000
smile and wave, and lower themselves back on the bed.]

00:36:40.000 --> 00:36:49.000
[Narrator:] By the age of five, the girls begin to get out of bed without any assistance, 

00:36:49.000 --> 00:37:04.000
and are able to remain in a vertical position for an extended period of time.

00:37:04.000 --> 00:37:09.000
[A nurse helps the girls to walk on the grass.]

00:37:09.000 --> 00:37:13.000
[Narrator:] The first attempts at movement were unsuccessful. 

00:37:13.000 --> 00:37:22.000
The segregated innervation of the legs could not provide a coordinated correlation between them.

00:37:22.000 --> 00:37:23.000
[The twins are in the exercise room.]

00:37:23.000 --> 00:37:25.000
[Each of the girls is holding a crutch, and they are successfully moving sideways.]

00:37:25.000 --> 00:37:26.000
[They hold their balance very well. ]

00:37:26.000 --> 00:37:28.000
[While the twins are leaning on a Swedish wall bar, Dasha lifts her leg and ties her shoe.]

00:37:28.000 --> 00:37:30.500
[Masha, in her turn, also demonstrates her balance and shoe-tying skill.]

00:37:30.500 --> 00:37:35.000
[Narrator:] The development of a walking function is of great scientific interest, 

00:37:35.000 --> 00:37:41.000
since it must be implemented by two separate bodies,

00:37:41.000 --> 00:37:49.000
where each of them is able to use only one leg for the above function.

00:37:49.000 --> 00:37:54.000
[The twins are lying on a mat, lifting their arms and legs in synchronized movements.]

00:37:54.000 --> 00:38:02.000
[The exercise is designed to help increase their movement coordination and help with motion.]

00:38:02.000 --> 00:38:04.000
[Narrator:] In order to teach the twins the walking function, 

00:38:04.000 --> 00:38:06.000
which for them is complicated, 

00:38:06.000 --> 00:38:09.000
and to develop a coordinated movement of the limbs 

00:38:09.000 --> 00:38:13.000
similar to the reciprocal coordination of a regular person, 

00:38:13.000 --> 00:38:17.000
the Central Institute of Prosthetics and Prosthesis Construction 

00:38:17.000 --> 00:38:21.000
has conducted difficult and persistent work, 

00:38:21.000 --> 00:38:37.000
advancing their motor skills.

00:38:37.000 --> 00:38:47.000
[The twins are riding a tricycle.]

00:38:47.000 --> 00:38:52.000
[Narrator:] Tricycle-riding, which requires synchronized pedal pushing, 

00:38:52.000 --> 00:38:55.000
was instrumental to the development of movement coordination

00:38:55.000 --> 00:39:00.190
between the two isolated nervous systems.

00:39:00.190 --> 00:39:02.000
[The twins are at the Swedish wall, holding crutches.]

00:39:02.000 --> 00:39:06.000
[They put the crutches aside and climb the wall.]

00:39:06.000 --> 00:39:09.000
[At the top the girls turn around, smile, then climb down, 

00:39:09.000 --> 00:39:10.000
where they retrieve their crutches and start walking forward.]

00:39:10.000 --> 00:39:11.000
[Narrator:] The Swedish wall bar exercises 

00:39:11.000 --> 00:39:39.000
also helped the children to synchronize their movements.

00:39:39.000 --> 00:39:55.000
[The girls on crutches walk forward with apparent ease.]

00:39:55.000 --> 00:40:02.000
[They stop and stand still, first on crutches, then without any support.]

00:40:02.000 --> 00:40:09.000
[Narrator:] During the walk the children effortlessly coordinate their movements. 

00:40:09.000 --> 00:40:18.000
They are able to walk with their eyes closed, and stand on the crutches

00:40:18.000 --> 00:40:34.000
without supporting each other with their free hands.

00:40:34.000 --> 00:40:43.000
They can even stand without crutches, while locking their knee joints with their arms.

00:40:43.000 --> 00:40:45.500
[The twins on crutches are standing on a stairway landing, 

00:40:45.500 --> 00:40:47.000
with a nurse behind them holding their hands.]

00:40:47.000 --> 00:40:49.000
[With the help of the nurse the girls start walking down the stairs.]

00:40:49.000 --> 00:40:53.000
[Narrator:] By then they learned to walk up and down the stairs, 

00:40:53.000 --> 00:41:10.000
though still with outside assistance.

00:41:10.000 --> 00:41:11.000
[The twins are sitting at the desk in a classroom.] 

00:41:11.000 --> 00:41:14.000
[A nurse shows them illustrations; the girls open their books and read.]

00:41:14.000 --> 00:41:17.000
[Later the girls are shown counting pencils, coloring pictures in books, and embroidering.]

00:41:17.000 --> 00:41:27.000
[Narrator:] Side by side with gymnastics, the girls are engaged in schoolwork. 

00:41:27.000 --> 00:41:35.000
The children's psychological development progresses normally and is consistent with their age. 

00:41:35.000 --> 00:43:06.000
They are learning how to read, to count, to color within the lines, and to embroider well. 

00:43:06.000 --> 00:43:10.000
And all of their craftwork is skillfully and neatly executed.

00:43:10.000 --> 00:43:12.000
[The twins are sitting at a large round table in the dining room, 

00:43:12.000 --> 00:43:13.000
accompanied by other children and adults.]

00:43:13.000 --> 00:43:14.000
[This is a celebration of the twins' seventh birthday. ]

00:43:14.000 --> 00:43:15.250
[A cake with the words 'Masha and Dasha are 7' is on the table in front of the girls.]

00:43:15.250 --> 00:43:16.500
[They all drink tea, eat fruit and pastries, and look very happy.]

00:43:16.500 --> 00:43:25.000
[Narrator:] In December 1957 Masha and Dasha turned seven. 

00:43:25.000 --> 00:43:34.000
Not only the children's peers came to wish them a happy birthday, but their adult friends as well, 

00:43:34.000 --> 00:43:40.000
the entire numerous and friendly staff of medical workers and researchers.

00:43:40.000 --> 00:43:41.000
[Two distinguished older men join the table, eat and talk to the twins.]

00:43:41.000 --> 00:43:45.000
[They are professors who work with the twins.]

00:43:45.000 --> 00:43:55.000
[Narrator:] Professor Anokhin and Professor Popov also came 

00:43:55.000 --> 00:44:04.000
to congratulate Masha and Dasha on their birthday.

00:44:04.000 --> 00:44:06.000
[The twins come to a coffee table with the gifts, look at them, and play with the toys.]

00:44:06.000 --> 00:44:09.000
[At the end they look up, smile and wave.]

00:44:09.000 --> 00:44:13.000
[Narrator:] Further development of the twins, mental as well as physical, will continue,

00:44:13.000 --> 00:44:19.000
without a doubt, to progress under the influence of both humoral and neural factors. 

00:44:19.000 --> 00:44:24.000
The former is stable, but the latter, in accordance with Masha's and Dasha's age,

00:44:24.000 --> 00:44:28.000
undergoes obvious differentiation. 

00:44:28.000 --> 00:44:33.000
Thus, it opens the promising prospects in future studies 

00:44:33.000 --> 00:44:39.150
for the assessment of neural and humoral factors in the mental life of the twins.

00:44:39.150 --> 00:44:45.182
The end.

